        <p class="eyebrow">MUSEOLOGY / MODULE 01</p>
        <h1>Introduction to Museology.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Understanding museums, their purpose, their functions
          and their evolving relationship with society.
        </p>
        <div class="lesson-meta">
          <span>FOUNDATION LEVEL</span>
          <span>INTRODUCTORY LESSON</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="lesson-layout">

        <aside class="lesson-sidebar">
          <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS LESSON</p>
          <nav>
            <a href="#objectives">Learning objectives</a>
            <a href="#meaning">What is Museology?</a>
            <a href="#museum">Understanding the museum</a>
            <a href="#history">Historical development</a>
            <a href="#functions">Functions of a museum</a>
            <a href="#society">Museums and society</a>
            <a href="#reflection">Think and reflect</a>
            <a href="#references">Further reading</a>
          </nav>
        </aside>

        <div class="lesson-content">

          <section id="objectives" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">01 / LEARNING OBJECTIVES</p>
            <h2>What will you learn?</h2>

            <p>By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:</p>

            <ul>
              <li>Explain the meaning and scope of museology.</li>
              <li>Understand the purpose and changing role of museums.</li>
              <li>Recognise important stages in the historical development of museums.</li>
              <li>Identify the major functions and responsibilities of museums.</li>
              <li>Understand the relationship between museums, collections and society.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section id="meaning" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">02 / UNDERSTANDING THE CONCEPT</p>
            <h2>What is Museology?</h2>

            <p>
              Museology is the study of museums, their history,
              purposes, functions, theories and relationships
              with society. It examines why museums exist,
              how they operate, what they collect and preserve,
              and how they communicate knowledge to their publics.
            </p>

            <p>
              It is an interdisciplinary field that draws upon
              history, archaeology, anthropology, art history,
              conservation, sociology, education and other
              areas of knowledge.
            </p>

            <p>
              Museology therefore goes beyond the practical
              management of museum collections. It also
              considers the ideas, values and responsibilities
              that shape museums as cultural institutions.
            </p>

            <div class="reference-note">
              <p class="note-label">A DISTINCTION TO REMEMBER</p>
              <p>
                Museology is concerned with the study and theory
                of museums, while museum practice involves the
                practical activities through which museums
                function, such as collection management,
                documentation, conservation and exhibition development.
              </p>
              <p>
                The two are closely connected: theoretical
                understanding informs professional practice,
                while practical experience continually raises
                new questions for museological study.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3>Why study Museology?</h3>

            <p>
              Museums are not simply places where objects are
              stored and displayed. They are institutions that
              select, interpret and communicate cultural and
              natural heritage.
            </p>

            <p>
              Studying museology helps us understand how
              collections acquire meaning, how knowledge
              is presented to the public, whose histories
              are represented and how museums respond
              to changing social expectations.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="museum" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">03 / THE MUSEUM AS AN INSTITUTION</p>
            <h2>What is a museum?</h2>

            <p>
              Museums have developed in different forms across
              cultures and historical periods. Their purposes
              and organisational structures may vary, but
              they share responsibilities towards heritage,
              knowledge and society.
            </p>

            <div class="reference-note">
              <p class="note-label">ICOM MUSEUM DEFINITION — 2022</p>
              <p>
                The International Council of Museums (ICOM)
                adopted a revised museum definition in 2022.
                It describes a museum as a permanent,
                not-for-profit institution serving society,
                engaged in researching, collecting, conserving,
                interpreting and exhibiting tangible and
                intangible heritage.
              </p>
              <p>
                The definition also emphasises accessibility,
                inclusion, diversity, sustainability,
                ethical practice and community participation.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://icom.museum/en/resources/standards-guidelines/museum-definition/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the complete ICOM museum definition ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <h3>Understanding museum collections</h3>

            <p>
              A museum collection is more than an accumulation
              of objects. It is an organised body of material
              and, in some contexts, intangible heritage
              assembled, documented and cared for according
              to the institution's purpose and collection policies.
            </p>

            <p>
              Objects may enter collections through acquisition,
              donation, bequest, transfer, fieldwork or other
              legally and ethically appropriate means.
            </p>

            <p>
              Their significance may be historical, artistic,
              scientific, archaeological, cultural, social
              or personal. Understanding that significance
              is central to responsible museum work.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="history" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">04 / HISTORICAL PERSPECTIVE</p>
            <h2>Historical development of museums.</h2>

            <p>
              The history of museums is not a single,
              continuous story. Different societies have
              developed traditions of collecting, preserving,
              displaying and transmitting knowledge in
              different ways.
            </p>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <span class="timeline-period">ANCIENT AND EARLY TRADITIONS</span>
              <h3>Early practices of collecting</h3>
              <p>
                Objects of religious, political, artistic
                and historical importance were collected,
                preserved and displayed in temples, palaces,
                treasuries and other settings. These practices
                demonstrate long-standing human interests
                in collecting and preserving significant objects,
                although they were not necessarily museums
                in the modern sense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <span class="timeline-period">RENAISSANCE AND EARLY MODERN PERIOD</span>
              <h3>Collections and cabinets of curiosity</h3>
              <p>
                European collectors assembled collections
                of natural specimens, artworks, antiquities
                and unusual objects. Known as cabinets
                of curiosities, these collections reflected
                interests in knowledge, classification,
                exploration and the natural world.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <span class="timeline-period">18TH AND 19TH CENTURIES</span>
              <h3>Emergence of public museums</h3>
              <p>
                During this period, museums increasingly
                developed as public institutions. Collections
                were organised and displayed for study,
                education and public engagement. National
                museums and specialised institutions became
                prominent in many parts of the world.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <span class="timeline-period">20TH CENTURY</span>
              <h3>Professionalisation and expansion</h3>
              <p>
                Museum work became increasingly specialised,
                with developments in conservation, documentation,
                collection management, exhibition design
                and museum education. Professional organisations
                and international cooperation also contributed
                to the development of museum standards and practice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <span class="timeline-period">CONTEMPORARY MUSEUMS</span>
              <h3>Participation, inclusion and new responsibilities</h3>
              <p>
                Contemporary museums increasingly engage
                with questions of representation, accessibility,
                community participation, restitution,
                sustainability and digital technologies.
                Their role continues to evolve in response
                to social, cultural and environmental changes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <p>
              These developments should not be understood
              as a universal sequence followed by every society.
              Museum histories differ across regions and
              cultural contexts.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="functions" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">05 / MUSEUM OPERATIONS</p>
            <h2>Major functions of a museum.</h2>

            <p>
              Museums undertake several interconnected
              functions. These activities support the
              preservation, understanding and communication
              of collections and heritage.
            </p>

            <div class="function-item">
              <span class="function-number">01</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Collection and acquisition</h3>
                <p>
                  Identifying, selecting and acquiring
                  objects or other forms of heritage
                  according to an institution's purpose,
                  policies and ethical responsibilities.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="function-item">
              <span class="function-number">02</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Documentation</h3>
                <p>
                  Recording information about objects,
                  including their identity, description,
                  provenance, history, condition and
                  collection movements.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="function-item">
              <span class="function-number">03</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Conservation and collection care</h3>
                <p>
                  Safeguarding collections through
                  preventive measures, examination,
                  research, appropriate treatments
                  and long-term collection management.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="function-item">
              <span class="function-number">04</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Research and interpretation</h3>
                <p>
                  Investigating collections and developing
                  knowledge about their materials, histories,
                  contexts and significance. Interpretation
                  communicates this knowledge to different
                  audiences.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="function-item">
              <span class="function-number">05</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Exhibition and display</h3>
                <p>
                  Presenting objects and ideas through
                  exhibitions, displays and other forms
                  of communication that help visitors
                  understand collections and their contexts.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="function-item">
              <span class="function-number">06</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Education and public engagement</h3>
                <p>
                  Creating opportunities for learning,
                  dialogue, participation and engagement
                  through educational programmes,
                  publications, workshops and other activities.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              These functions are interconnected.
              For example, an exhibition depends on
              collection research, documentation,
              conservation, interpretation and
              appropriate display planning.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="society" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">06 / CONTEMPORARY PERSPECTIVES</p>
            <h2>Museums and society.</h2>

            <p>
              Museums operate within society and are shaped
              by the communities, histories and cultural
              contexts around them. They do not simply
              preserve the past; they also influence how
              the past is understood in the present.
            </p>

            <p>
              Decisions about what museums collect,
              preserve, display and interpret can influence
              public understanding of history, identity
              and cultural heritage.
            </p>

            <p>
              Contemporary museum practice therefore
              involves questions such as:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>Whose histories and experiences are represented?</li>
              <li>How can museums engage with local and indigenous communities?</li>
              <li>How should museums address contested histories and questions of provenance?</li>
              <li>How can collections and exhibitions become more accessible?</li>
              <li>What responsibilities do museums have towards future generations?</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="reference-note">
              <p class="note-label">A CONTEMPORARY PERSPECTIVE</p>
              <p>
                UNESCO's 2015 Recommendation concerning
                the Protection and Promotion of Museums
                and Collections recognises museums as
                contributors to education, cultural diversity,
                social cohesion and sustainable development.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/recommendation-concerning-protection-and-promotion-museums-and-collections-their-diversity-and-their"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the UNESCO Recommendation ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <p>
              Understanding museums therefore requires
              us to consider not only their collections
              and operations, but also their social
              responsibilities and relationships with
              the people they serve.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="reflection" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">07 / THINK AND REFLECT</p>
            <h2>Consider the situation.</h2>

            <div class="reflection">
              <p class="scenario-label">A MUSEUM SCENARIO</p>
              <h3>
                A museum is planning an exhibition
                about the history of a local community.
              </h3>
              <p>
                The museum has a collection of objects
                associated with the community. However,
                the collection records contain limited
                information about the people who made,
                used or donated these objects.
              </p>
              <p>
                The museum wants to develop an exhibition
                that represents the community's history
                accurately and meaningfully.
              </p>
              <p>
                What should the museum consider before
                developing the exhibition?
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3>Questions for reflection</h3>

            <ol>
              <li>What additional information should the museum investigate?</li>
              <li>How could community members contribute to the research and interpretation?</li>
              <li>Why is documentation important in understanding collection objects?</li>
              <li>What responsibilities does the museum have when representing a community's history?</li>
              <li>How can the exhibition become accessible and meaningful to different audiences?</li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              There is no single answer to this situation.
              The purpose is to recognise that museum work
              involves research, collaboration, ethical
              consideration and public responsibility.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="references" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">08 / REFERENCES AND FURTHER READING</p>
            <h2>Continue exploring.</h2>

            <div class="reference-item">
              <h3>International Council of Museums (ICOM), 2022</h3>
              <p>
                Museum Definition. The internationally
                adopted definition of a museum, including
                its purpose, functions and responsibilities
                towards society.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://icom.museum/en/resources/standards-guidelines/museum-definition/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the ICOM Museum Definition ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <div class="reference-item">
              <h3>UNESCO, 2015</h3>
              <p>
                Recommendation concerning the Protection
                and Promotion of Museums and Collections,
                their Diversity and their Role in Society.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/recommendation-concerning-protection-and-promotion-museums-and-collections-their-diversity-and-their"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the UNESCO Recommendation ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <div class="reference-item">
              <h3>International Council of Museums (ICOM), 2026</h3>
              <p>
                Revised ICOM Code of Ethics for Museums.
                An updated professional framework for
                museum responsibilities and ethical practice,
                adopted in June 2026.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://icom.museum/en/resources/standards-guidelines/code-of-ethics/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Explore the ICOM Code of Ethics ↗
